L["What the item typically sells for right now. From full listing data it's the average of the cheapest 15-30% of the supply on the auction house, which ignores a few cheap or absurd listings. From a browse summary (what many scanners do by default) only the cheapest listing is known, so then it's the lowest price."] = true
L["What the path nets under each goal (the one in use is marked)."] = true
L["What the whole market's daily sales of an item would earn at this recipe's profit: profit per craft times how many sell a day. It's the size of the opportunity, not your income - you'd sell a share of those, after the other sellers, and a recipe with a cooldown (transmutes) only as often as it allows: the sidebar says how often. Use it to compare recipes; a big Market/day with few sellers is the best kind."] = true
L["What to post at so it sells within the listing time - instead of undercutting the cheapest listing by 1c. Buyers take the cheapest listings first, so AME counts the units ahead of you at each price, how fast the item sells, and how often new listings undercut you, and weighs the chance of selling against the deposit you lose if it doesn't. How far the item's price swings in a day - from your scans of it, else its 30-day volatility - sets the choices apart: Quick goes under where the cheapest listing usually dips within a few hours, Normal is the best price for the chance, Patient waits for the usual peak within the listing time (nothing above it is suggested). Quick only shows when it's cheaper than Normal. It learns every item's pattern from your scans (busy hours, weekdays, how hard sellers compete) and from your own posts: if items keep expiring at its prices it gets more careful for that item. With little data it borrows from similar items; the bars show how much it knows."] = true
L["What you could make if one thing were not missing: a reagent, or a tool. Sorted by profit."] = true
L["What you paid per unit for the units you bought on the auction house (oldest purchases are sold first). Items you looted or crafted have no cost."] = true
L["What you've queued - from a profession window, Top earners, Sell or refine or Leveling - worked out into steps, parts first, with its profit and what's to buy in the heading. A part's Buy buys it instead of making it (Make on its material makes it again); x takes a queued craft off the queue, and Clear empties it. To do lists what to do in order: what to buy, what to fetch from the bank, the mailbox or your other characters, then what to craft and on whom. Missing to shopping list puts everything still missing on the Craft queue shopping list."] = true
L["What's left to buy"] = true
L["When"] = true
L["When a vendor sells something one of your shopping lists still needs (thread, vials, flux...), a For your lists panel opens beside the merchant window: how many you need, what that buys in the vendor's stacks, and the cost. Buy takes one row, Buy all everything you can afford. The rows count down as the items reach your bags, and the panel isn't there at vendors with nothing you need."] = true
L["When one of your characters knows a recipe that makes it into something worth more (silk cloth into silk bandages, say), the item page says so instead: make that first, then vendor or post it."] = true
L["When that step belongs to another of this character's professions, Craft next opens that profession's window on it: click Craft next again to craft it."] = true
